// Raw-ingredient content — what the factory's *consumables* cost
// (GAMEPLAY.md §5's first cost axis: "ingredients — recurring, scales
// directly with production volume"). Data, never hardcoded in sim_core,
// exactly like `recipes.ron`, `machines.ron`, `shop.ron` and
// `contracts.ron`; the loader is `sim_core::sim::ingredients::
// IngredientCatalog`.
//
// `schema_version` follows ARCHITECTURE.md §4's data-file versioning rule:
// the loader rejects a mismatched version rather than silently misreading a
// changed shape, the same way every other data file's loader does.
//
// WHY THIS IS A SECOND FILE AND NOT A COLUMN IN `shop.ron`. They price two
// different *kinds of thing*, and the difference is not cosmetic. A
// `shop.ron` entry prices a **buildable kind** — something you buy once,
// place on the floor, and get back if you pick it up; buying one credits
// `OwnedEquipment`. An entry here prices a **consumable item or fluid** —
// something you buy by the unit, load into a hopper or a tank, and never
// see again once a machine has eaten it; buying one credits
// `OwnedIngredients`. The two ledgers are separate for the same reason
// `OwnedEquipment` is not an `Inventory`: they share a shape and not a
// meaning, and one file with a "kind" discriminator would only make the
// distinction a runtime check instead of a type.
//
// WHAT COUNTS AS RAW. An id here is an item or fluid that some recipe in
// `data/recipes.ron` *consumes* and no recipe *produces* — tomato, sugar,
// spice mix and molasses on the solid side, vinegar and liquid smoke on the
// fluid side. Everything else in the chain (tomato paste, the liquid mixes,
// the cooked sauces, bottles, boxes) is made by the factory and therefore is
// never bought. **Finished ketchup is the clearest case**: the BBQ mixer
// consumes it, so it looks like an input, but the cooking vat produces it,
// so it is not raw and has no price here — a BBQ branch buys ketchup by
// building enough line to make it. `IngredientCatalog::
// validate_covers` checks that direction against the shipped recipe file, so
// a new raw ingredient with no price is caught by a test rather than
// discovered as a hopper that can never be filled. It is deliberately NOT a
// `World`-construction panic — an unpriced ingredient leaves the world
// perfectly coherent, it just cannot be bought, which is the same call
// `contracts.rs` made about an unsatisfiable contract.
//
// SOLIDS AND FLUIDS SHARE ONE LEDGER, ON PURPOSE. `ItemId` and `FluidId`
// are both plain `String` aliases (`sim/types.rs`), the ids never collide,
// and what the player is buying in both cases is *stock measured in whole
// units*. One catalog and one ledger is therefore one mechanism, not two
// that have to be kept in step — and a shipment of vinegar is no more a
// different kind of purchase than a shipment of sugar.
//
// PRICES ARE PLACEHOLDERS, like `shop.ron`'s (GAMEPLAY.md §10 puts real
// balance in a future pass). What they encode is a shape:
//
//   1. One boxed ketchup costs 4 tomato + 1 sugar + 2 vinegar to make,
//      i.e. 4 + 3 + 4 = 11 in ingredients.
//   2. The contract board pays between 24 and 40 per box of ketchup. So
//      ingredients are roughly a third of revenue: a running factory is
//      comfortably profitable, but overproducing into a delivery pool no
//      contract wants is a real waste of money rather than a free
//      accumulation.
//   3. Tomatoes are the cheapest per unit and are consumed four at a time,
//      which makes them the biggest line on the bill — bulk produce
//      behaving like bulk produce. Sugar is the dearest per unit (it is
//      refined) and the smallest line.
//   4. **One boxed BBQ holds the same shape, and it inherits half its
//      bill.** It costs 1 ketchup + 1 spice mix + 1 molasses + 1 liquid
//      smoke, and finished ketchup is not raw, so the arithmetic recurses:
//      11 (the ketchup a box of BBQ eats) + 5 + 4 + 3 = **23 per box**, of
//      which 11 is the ketchup. Its contracts pay 60-65 per box, so the
//      ratio lands in the same third-of-revenue band the ketchup line sits
//      in — which is the point. Converting a line's output to BBQ is worth
//      roughly 40% more revenue for one extra mixer, one extra vat and four
//      more heads; good enough to be the obvious next move, not so good
//      that a ketchup-only board becomes a mistake.
//
// That recursion is also why the BBQ raws are priced where they are rather
// than by their own flavour logic: the three of them come to 12, which is
// about what the ketchup they are blended into costs, so a box of BBQ is
// half inherited sauce and half new ingredients — legible on the bill, and
// it keeps the BBQ margin from running away from the ketchup margin.
// As in `data/recipes.ron`: an entry may carry an optional `display_name`,
// and this extension lets it be written as its value rather than as
// `Some(...)`. No shipped raw needs one — every id here title-cases
// correctly — but the field is here for the one that does not.
#![enable(implicit_some)]
(
    schema_version: 1,
    entries: [
        // The chain's bulk raw produce. Cheapest per unit and consumed four
        // at a time by the crusher.
        IngredientEntry(
            id: "tomato",
            price: 1,
            blurb: "The chain's bulk produce, and the cheapest thing on the shelf per unit — which is just as well, because the crusher eats them several to the pail.",
            icon: "item_tomato",
        ),
        // Refined, so the dearest of the three per unit — but the mixer only
        // takes one per batch.
        IngredientEntry(
            id: "sugar",
            price: 3,
            blurb: "Refined, so it costs more per unit than tomatoes do, but the mixer takes very little of it per batch.",
            icon: "item_sugar",
        ),
        // The one raw ingredient that is a fluid rather than an item: it is
        // bought by the unit exactly like the two solids and loaded into a
        // `fluid_tank` instead of a hopper.
        IngredientEntry(
            id: "vinegar",
            price: 2,
            blurb: "The first raw that is a fluid rather than a solid: bought by the unit like everything else, but loaded into a fluid tank instead of a hopper, and delivered to the mixer down a pipe.",
            icon: "item_vinegar",
        ),

        // --- The BBQ branch's three raws ---
        // BBQ buys no tomatoes: its bulk input is finished ketchup, which
        // the factory already makes (`data/recipes.ron`). What it buys is
        // the three things that turn ketchup into something else.

        // The dearest raw in the game, and the one that carries the flavour:
        // a blended spice is bought already ground and mixed, so it prices
        // like a refined good rather than produce. One per batch.
        IngredientEntry(
            id: "spice_mix",
            price: 5,
            blurb: "Bought already ground and blended, which is why it prices like a refined good rather than like produce. The dearest raw in the game, and the one that makes BBQ taste of anything.",
            icon: "item_spice_mix",
        ),
        // The sweetener BBQ uses where ketchup uses sugar — unrefined, so a
        // shade cheaper per unit than sugar, and likewise one per batch.
        IngredientEntry(
            id: "molasses",
            price: 4,
            blurb: "What BBQ uses where ketchup uses sugar. Unrefined, so a shade cheaper per unit than sugar — and it arrives on a belt like any other solid.",
            icon: "item_molasses",
        ),
        // The second raw fluid, and the reason a BBQ branch needs a
        // `fluid_tank` of its own: the BBQ cook draws it straight into the
        // vat alongside the piped mix. Cheapest of the three — it is bought
        // by the drum and used a unit at a time.
        IngredientEntry(
            id: "liquid_smoke",
            price: 3,
            blurb: "The second raw fluid, and the reason a BBQ branch needs a fluid tank of its own: the cook draws it straight into the vat alongside the piped mix.",
            icon: "item_liquid_smoke",
        ),
    ],
)
